The API map, verified shapes:

```js
// SELECT
const el = document.querySelector('#app .item');      // first match
const all = document.querySelectorAll('.item');        // static NodeList
const byId = document.getElementById('app');          // fastest, id only
all.forEach(...)  // NodeList supports forEach (no spread needed)

// CREATE + INSERT
const li = document.createElement('li');
li.textContent = 'New item';                 // textContent: no HTML parsed — safe
li.className = 'item active';
list.append(li);                              // also: prepend, before, after

// REMOVE + REPLACE
li.remove();                                  // no parent traversal needed
el.replaceChildren(...newEls);                // idiom: clear + set in one op
el.innerHTML = '';                            // legacy clear

// THE EFFICIENCY PATTERNS
// 1. Build OFF-DOM, insert ONCE (each insert can reflow):
const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
items.forEach(i => { const li = ...; frag.append(li); });
list.append(frag);            // ONE DOM mutation, not N

// 2. Delegation for dynamic children (covered in its own question)
// 3. classList over className string surgery:
el.classList.add('on', 'visible'); el.classList.toggle('dark');

// 4. Security: textContent over innerHTML for ANY user-derived content
// innerHTML = '<li>' + userInput + '</li>'  ← XSS: userInput can close the tag
```

The reflow discipline (why the patterns exist): every mutation may invalidate layout; the browser batches and defers — UNLESS you force it by reading geometry (offsetHeight, getBoundingClientRect) after a pending write. Inserting a built fragment once keeps invalidation to a single reflow; inserting in a loop with reads between iterations is layout thrashing (verified mechanism in the render-pipeline question).
